    "methodology": "10,000-game solitaire heuristic. Sequencing: land drop, cheapest ramp, commander on affordability, biggest affordable threat, then other proactive spells. Mana is spent once per turn: the turn's pool is built after the land drop and each cast removes the sources it paid with, so the whole turn's casts (plus the combo and engine-activation gates, which are checked against what is left) never exceed the available mana (DEC-937). Sources that come online untapped mid-turn join the pool. Meaningful board = pressure score >= 8 or 2+ threats. Lethal proxy = cumulative combat-pressure proxy >= 40; combat pressure uses mana-value-based creature scoring because card metadata omits power/toughness. Combo lethal = at least one winConditions[] entry tagged kind:'combo' has all pieces present (hand, battlefield, or substitutable) and combined mana cost payable. Substitution priority for missing pieces: (1) bargain free-cast tutor (e.g. Beseech the Mirror) when a non-rock enchantment is on battlefield OR commander has been in play for 1+ turn (approximates Sauron-style amass token from real-game opponent casts); (2) type-matched regular tutor whose tutor_targets includes piece's card type and tutor_mv_cap permits piece's MV; (3) reanimation spell (decklist[].primary_role/secondary_role == 'recursion') for permanent pieces only \u2014 cost replaces piece's cast cost (treats piece as if in graveyard). Each substitute is consumed once per combo line. Tutors, non-permanent combo pieces, and reanimation spells are reserved from filler casting. v2 limitations: no literal graveyard tracking (reanim assumes piece could be in GY), no opponent interaction, no multi-turn assembly tracking, no stack-trigger ordering. Commander hatch engine modeled (DEC-938): with the commander on the battlefield and at least one instant-speed sacrifice outlet (3 free/unlimited, 1 once-per-untap) online, each Egg that dies reveals from the top of the library until a creature card and puts it onto the battlefield free \u2014 a put, not a cast, so it credits impact, combat and draw like a cast but never re-triggers a cast-gated model. Egg supply per own turn: commander activations paid at 2 generic out of what the turn's casts left, one per untap and at most 4 before doublers (Thornbite Staff untaps her per death, but only while Ashnod's Altar is out to pay the next activation and only if the Staff was on the battlefield when she entered), plus entry Eggs (Palani's Hatcher's two, doubled again by Elesh Norn) and one per land drop from a landfall Egg maker the turn after it lands. Each of 2 token doublers doubles every Egg; each of 2 trigger doublers adds one hatch per death. Skullclamp equips own-turn Eggs at 1 generic each and credits 2 cards. Seedborn Muse adds 3 free activations on the opponents' untap steps (instant-speed outlets only; those creatures are ready next turn regardless of haste). Attack-triggered hatches (Raph & Mikey) enter attacking and are ready the same combat. Hard cap 8 hatches per turn \u2014 the Thornbite infinite is scored by the combo systems, not here. Floors: no blockers or opponents, Eggs left over never block or trade, the Dinosaur and Dragon tokens that Egg sacrifices leave behind are not credited, Skullclamp and Palani's Hatcher do not open the engine on their own, ramp-fetched lands do not trigger landfall, and Maskwood Nexus turning every creature into an Egg, the paid Zopandrel/Mondrak outlets and opponent-end-step hatching beyond Seedborn are unmodelled. Team-haste rule modeled (DEC-938): the combat profile ignores printed Haste fleet-wide, so while any of 3 static haste permanents (or both halves of 1 configured pair) is on the battlefield, a creature that enters \u2014 cast or hatched \u2014 is combat-ready the turn it entered, as are 1 printed-haste creatures. Floor: static sources also grant haste to creatures already on the battlefield when the source lands, which this entry-time rule misses."
